% ============================================================================
% mineral-hardness — the ten REFERENCE MINERALS of the Mohs hardness scale and
% the whole-number hardness each one is assigned (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first quantitative facts of mineralogy, and a rung a GEOLOGY /
% EARTH-SCIENCE agent reasons UP from: how hard a mineral is — whether it can be
% scratched by your fingernail, a copper coin, a steel knife, or nothing short of
% a diamond — is pinned to a ladder of ten well-known reference minerals, each
% given a definite number from 1 (softest) to 10 (hardest). "Talc is 1, the
% softest; diamond is 10, the hardest; quartz sits at 7, right in the middle."
% Before you can reason about whether one mineral scratches another (a higher
% Mohs number always scratches a lower one) you must first know each reference
% mineral's number. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? mineral_hardness(quartz, $H)      % 7
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `mineral_hardness(mineral, hardness)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% number WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% mineral the scale does not fix (it never invents a hardness).
%
% The ten reference minerals, as a little truth table (softest at the top,
% hardest at the bottom — the classic Mohs ladder):
%
%     mineral       hardness   a beginner's cue
%     -----------   --------   ------------------------------------------------
%     talc            1        softest of all — a fingernail crushes it
%     gypsum          2        still scratched by a fingernail
%     calcite         3        scratched by a copper coin
%     fluorite        4        scratched by a steel knife
%     apatite         5        just barely scratched by a knife
%     orthoclase      6        scratches window glass
%     quartz          7        the middle of the scale; scratches glass easily
%     topaz           8        harder than most common minerals
%     corundum        9        second only to diamond (ruby and sapphire)
%     diamond        10        hardest natural material — scratches everything
%
% Because each `hardness` is a NUMBER (not a label), a recalled value composes
% straight into a comparison: mineral A scratches mineral B exactly when
% hardness(A) > hardness(B), so the recalled numbers ARE the ordering that drives
% a scratch-test reasoner. That is the concrete bridge from RECALL to COMPUTE.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a number and recall the mineral that has it:
%
%     ? mineral_hardness($M, 10)   % diamond — reverse recall
%
% A mineral that is NOT one of these ten reference minerals — `gold`, `graphite`,
% `mica` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a hardness recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a number. (Those minerals have real Mohs hardnesses, but this
% table fixes ONLY the ten defining reference minerals of the scale itself, so it
% answers exactly those and abstains on everything else.) That honest-abstention
% property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on.
%
% Each `hardness` is copied character-for-character from the NPS sentence that
% states it — never a number the source does not print for that mineral.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every mineral→hardness pair
% below was read out of a fetched U.S. National Park Service page this session,
% and any pair that could not be grounded there would have been dropped). All ten
% numbers come from the SAME primary U.S. government source, the National Park
% Service article "Mohs Hardness Scale" (nps.gov), which lists every reference
% mineral with its number in a single sentence, quoted here character-for-
% character so any reader can re-check it:
%
%   "Diamond, 10; Corundum, 9; Topaz, 8; Quartz, 7; Orthoclase, 6; Apatite, 5;
%    Flourite, 4; Calcite, 3; Gypsum, 2; and Talc, 1."
%
% That one span fixes all ten rows at once. (Two spelling notes, so the atoms
% stay checkable against the quote: the NPS page prints "Flourite", a typo for
% the standard "Fluorite" — the row key uses the standard spelling `fluorite`
% while the number 4 it is bound to comes verbatim from the source; and the
% source names the hardness-6 mineral "Orthoclase", the specific feldspar Mohs
% used, so the key is `orthoclase`.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold that single sentence — the one NPS span that fixes every
% row. The source is a primary U.S. government NPS page (nps.gov), so `trust
% authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table mineral_hardness {
    columns mineral, hardness

    row (talc, 1)
    row (gypsum, 2)
    row (calcite, 3)
    row (fluorite, 4)
    row (apatite, 5)
    row (orthoclase, 6)
    row (quartz, 7)
    row (topaz, 8)
    row (corundum, 9)
    row (diamond, 10)

    source "Diamond, 10; Corundum, 9; Topaz, 8; Quartz, 7; Orthoclase, 6; Apatite, 5; Flourite, 4; Calcite, 3; Gypsum, 2; and Talc, 1."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/articles/mohs-hardness-scale.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
